"Internal Error ()\nciteturn5view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://static-content.springer-cdn.com/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41467-020-17126-3/MediaObjects/41467_2020_17126_MOESM1_ESM.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://static-content.springer-cdn.com/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41467-020-17126-3/MediaObjects/41467_2020_17126_MOESM1_ESM.pdf is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-76366-x\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-76366-x: (500) Redirected to https://idp.nature.com/authorize?response_type=cookie&client_id=grover&redirect_uri=https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-76366-x which is not accessible via this tool. (non-retryable error)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPhase Transformation of Lithium‐rich Oxide Cathode in Full Cell and its Suppression by Solid Electrolyte Interphase on Graphite Anode - Tu - 2020 - ENERGY & ENVIRONMENTAL MATERIALS - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/eem2.12034)\nciteturn5view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"pattern\":\"200 mA\"}); Total lines: 697\nL286: A typical lithium-rich oxide, Li_{1.2}Mn_{0.55}Ni_{0.15}Co_{0.1}O_{2} (LRO), was prepared as reported in our previous works.【39†34】, 【98†35】 The slurries of LRO, carbon black, and polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) (by weight ratio of 8:1:1) were coated on Al foil as the positive electrode, and the slurries of graphite, super-P, and PVDF (on weight ratio of 9:0.5:0.5) on Cu foil as the negative electrode.\nL287: The average active material loading densities of LRO and graphite electrodes were ~3.9 and ~2.2 mg • cm^{−2}, respectively. The standard electrolyte (STD) consists of 1 m LiPF_{6} dissolved in the mixed solvents of ethylene carbonate (EC), ethyl methyl carbonate (EMC), and diethyl carbonate (DEC) (on weight ratio = 3:5:2, provided by Guangzhou Tianci Materials Technology Co. Ltd., China).\nL288: The additive-containing electrolyte consists of 2 wt.% Prop-1-ene-1,3-sultone (PES, purchased from Tokyo Chemical Industry Shanghai Co. Ltd., China) dissolved in STD electrolyte. The graphite/LRO full cells were assembled with a capacity ratio of 1/1.3 for cathode/anode based on 200 mA•h• g^{−1} for LRO and 350 mAh g^{−1} for graphite.\nL289: The full cells were constructed of LRO cathode, Celgard 2300 separator, and graphite anode in 2025-coin cells, and the half cells were assembled with LRO or graphite in similar configurations with Li metal as counter electrode in 2025-coin cells. All coin cells were assembled in the glove box, and the concentrations of water and oxygen were <0.1 ppm.\nL290: #### 4.2 Electrochemical Measurements and Physical Characterizations\nL291: The charge/discharge tests were conducted on a Land cell test system (Land CT2001, China). All full-cell cycling tests adopted the following protocol: the full cells were firstly charged to 4.6 V and then discharged to 2 V at constant current (CC) at 0.05 C rate (1 C = 200 mA• g^{−1}) for the initial three cycles, then be charged/discharged (CC) at 0.5 C rate between 2 and 4.6 V for the subsequent cycles.\nL292: The Li/LRO and Li/graphite half cells were charged/discharged at 0.1 and 0.05 C for the initial three cycles and at 0.5 and 0.2 C rates (1 C = 200 mA• g^{−1}) for the subsequent cycles. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was performed on Solartron 1480 instrument (England) at 5 mV amplitude from 100 kHz to 0.01 Hz, with measurements carried out in the full discharge condition (~2 V).\nL293: The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) was performed on Solartron 1480 at 0.1 mV• s^{−1} from open circuit potential (OCP) to 0 V (vs. Li^{+}/Li) in 2025-coin cells that were assembled with stainless steel plate coated with gold as working electrode, Li metal plate as counter, and reference electrodes and Celgard 2300 separator.\nL294: Physical characterizations were performed to observe the surface and structure evolutions of electrodes. The morphology and structure of electrodes were analyzed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, JEM-6510, Japan) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM-2100HR, Japan). The distribution of elements on electrode surfaces was observed by energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS, Bruker Dual Quantax 200) with a signal area size of 10 × 10 μm.\nL295: The chemical compositions on electrode surfaces were also analyzed by XPS on Thermo Fisher Scientific (UK) with an Al Kα X-ray source. The data were collected from 0 to 1350 eV with a signal spot size of 400 μm. The binder energy was calibrated based on C1s peak at 284.8 eV. The crystal structure of LRO was evaluated by X-ray diffraction (XRD, Bruker D8 Advance, Germany) performed at 60 kV and 80 mA using Cu Ka radiation (aλ = 1.5405 nm) between the 2θ range from 10 to 80°.\nL296: Because of the high sensitivity to the crystal chemistry and related phase structure transformation, Raman spectroscopy (WITec GmbH) was used to monitor the crystal structure evolution of LRO using green laser at 523 nm, achieving a diffraction-limit spot size with 500 nm in diameter and 1 μm in depth length. Five tested spots were taken on each sample to ensure reproducibility.\nL297: ## Acknowledgements\nL298: \nL299: This work is supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant No. 21872058) and the Key Project of Science and Technology in Guangdong Province (2017A010106006).\nL300: \nL301: ## Conflict of Interest\nL302: \nL303: The authors declare no conflict of interest.\nL304: ##\nL305: \nL306: Supporting Information\nL307: \nL308: Filename  | Description\nL309: --- | ---\nL310: 【99†eem212034-sup-0001-Supinfo.docx】Word document, 15.6 MB  |\nL311: \nL312: Please note: The publisher is not responsible for the content or functionality of any supporting information supplied by the authors. Any queries (other than missing content) should be directed to the corresponding author for the article.\nL313: ## 【17†References】\nL314: \nL315:   * 1 K. Xu, Chem. Rev. 2004, 104, 4303.\nL316: \nL317: 【100†CAS】 【101†PubMed】 【102†Web of Science®】 【103†Google Scholar】\nL318: \nL319:   * 2 D. Andre, S.-J. Kim, P. Lamp, S. F. Lux, F. Maglia, O. Paschos, B. Stiaszny, J. Mater. Chem. A 2015, 3, 6709.\nL320: \nL321: 【104†CAS】 【105†Web of Science®】 【106†Google Scholar】\nL322: \nL323:   * 3 L. Croguennec, M. R. Palacin, J. Am. Chem. Soc. 2015, 137, 3140.\nL324: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPhase Transformation of Lithium‐rich Oxide Cathode in Full Cell and its Suppression by Solid Electrolyte Interphase on Graphite Anode - Tu - 2020 - ENERGY & ENVIRONMENTAL MATERIALS - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/eem2.12034)\nciteturn5view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"pattern\":\"1C\"}); Total lines: 697\nNo matching text found for \"1C\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe major updates from BatPaC v4.0 to BatPaC v5.0 are as follows: (https://publications.anl.gov/anlpubs/2022/07/176234.pdf)\nciteturn5view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 146; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view2\",\"pattern\":\"Table 2.1\"}); Total lines: 4457\nL57@P3: 1.3 Overview of BatPaC Model................................................................................. 2\nL58@P3: 2. Battery Design for BatPaC..................................................................................................... 4\nL59@P3: 2.1 Overview of Battery Design ................................................................................ 4\nL60@P3: 2.2 Cell Design........................................................................................................... 6\nL65@P3: 3.1 Background and General Instructions................................................................ 12\nL66@P3: 3.2 Dashboard Worksheet........................................................................................ 13\nL67@P3: 3.2.1 Dashboard Ribbon ............................................................................................. 13\nL68@P3: 3.2.2 Dashboard Inputs............................................................................................... 15\nL89@P4: 5.2.1 Governing Equations ......................................................................................... 48\nL90@P4: 5.2.2 ASI Model.......................................................................................................... 50\nL91@P4: 5.2.3 Model Solution................................................................................................... 57\nL92@P4: 5.2.4 Additional Constraints....................................................................................... 57\nL129@P6: 9. Manufacturing Cost ........................................................................................................... 100\nL130@P6: 9.1 Approach.......................................................................................................... 100\nL131@P6: 9.2 Materials Costs, Purchased Items, and Pack Integration ................................. 100\nL132@P6: 9.2.1 Battery Specific Materials Costs...................................................................... 100\nL538@P21: design and performance modeling. Section 6 describes how BatPaC adjusts the design to account\nL539@P21: for electric vehicle charging. Section 7 details the battery management system calculations.\nL540@P21: Section 8 provides information on the thermal management system. Section 9 discusses the\nL541@P21: manufacturing cost calculations. Finally, section 10 describes an accounting system used to keep\nL542@P21-22: track of the recoverable materials at the end of life and during manufacturing of the pack.4\nL543@P22: 2. BATTERY DESIGN FOR BATPAC\nL544@P22: This section presents the key aspects to the battery pack design along with key terminology for\nL545@P22: using BatPaC effectively. It is suggested that one read this section before proceeding to the next\nL546@P22: sections.\nL547@P22: 2.1 Overview of Battery Design\nL548@P22: A battery pack for an electrified vehicle consists of numerous cells linked together in combinations\nL549@P22: of series and parallel configurations to reach the desired pack voltage, energy, and power\nL550@P22: requirements. BatPaC calculates the mass, size, and cost of a battery pack assuming the pack is\nL551@P22: composed of modules containing stiff pouch cells. An overview of the elements in the battery pack\nL552@P22: is provided in Figure 2.1. Details on the components are provided in the next subsections.\nL553@P22: The cell is the basic building block of the pack. It contains all the active materials. BatPaC assumes\nL554@P22: a stiff pouch cell, which indicates the type of enclosure and form-factor of the cell. The next level\nL555@P22: is the module, which contains multiple cells. The cells may be connected in combinations of series\nL556@P22: or parallel. The module also contains aluminum heat conductors wrapped around each cell for\nL557@P22: thermal management, a module management control system, terminals, and an enclosure. The\nL558@P22: module in Figure 2.1 is open on one side to show the cells stacked inside. The module is fully\nL559@P22: enclosed around the cells in the BatPaC design.\nL560@P22: Next, BatPaC assumes that multiple modules are placed within a row rack. The row rack provides\nL561@P22: structural support to maintain compression on the modules. It also includes cooling panels to\nL562@P22: manage the heat generated by the cells in the modules. Finally, BatPaC assumes all row racks are\nL563@P22: connected to form the entire pack. The full pack includes the full battery management system and\nL564@P22: additional features for the thermal management system. Bus bars and terminals are also included\nL565@P22: for drawing power to and from the pack. Rows of modules and modules within rows can be\nL566@P22: connected in combinations of series and parallel to achieve the desired pack voltage. Note that\nL567@P22: unique design decisions will result in modifications to the generic pack structure described herein\nL568@P22: – i.e., packs containing only one module or one row of modules. Details on how BatPaC\nL569@P22-23: accommodates these unique designs are provided in Section 5.5\nL570@P23: Figure 2.1. Overview of the pack design assumed by BatPaC v5.0. Packs are composed of rows\nL571@P23: of modules, which contain stiff pouch-cell batteries.\nL572@P23: BatPaC contains four default vehicle types – mild hybrid-electric (mildHEV), high-power hybrid\nL573@P23: electric (HEV-HP), plug-in hybrid-electric (PHEV), and all-electric (EV) vehicles. BatPaC applies\nL574@P23: the same general pack format presented in Figure 2.1 for all vehicle types. That is, all vehicle packs\nL575@P23: are composed of modules containing stiff pouch cells. BatPaC modifies the cell and pack designs\nL576@P23: to account for different features required by the batteries in these vehicles. An overview of the\nL577@P23: important features for each vehicle type is provided in Table 2.1. Details on how the BatPaC\nL578@P23: calculation is modified to account for these features can be found in Sections 4.2 (overview), 4.3\nL579@P23-24: (pack configuration), 5.7 (design inputs), and 9.2.3 (cost).6\nL580@P24: Table 2.1. Overview of battery features accounted for by BatPaC\nL581@P24: Battery related features for selected\nL582@P24: vehicles mildHEV HEV-HP PHEV EV\nL583@P24: Start/stop system ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓\nL584@P24: Regenerative braking  ✓ ✓ ✓\nL585@P24: Pulse power for short acceleration  ✓ ✓ ✓\nL586@P24: Charge depleting   ✓ ✓\nL587@P24: Rechargeable   ✓ ✓\nL588@P24: Pure electric    ✓\nL589@P24: Power requirements (kW) 10 – 40 50 – 250 75 – 300 100 – 800\nL590@P24: Energy requirements (kWh) ~1 1 – 10 10 – 20 50 – 200\nL591@P24: 2.2 Cell Design\nL592@P24: BatPaC uses a stiff pouch for the cell format. A full explanation of the cell sizing is provided in\nL593@P24: Section 5.2. A schematic showing the main features of the cell is shown in Figure 2.2. The cell is\nL594@P24: comprised of positive and negative electrodes coated on current collectors with separators placed\nL595@P24: between the layers. Figure 2.2a shows a schematic of a single bi-cell layer within the pouch and a\nL596@P24: detailed view is shown in Figure 2.3. The bi-cell layer contains enough material to make two unit\u0002cells – i.e., one positive foil coated on both sides with positive electrodes, one negative foil coated\nL597@P24: on both sides with negative electrodes, and two separators. The positive and negative electrodes\nL598@P24: are composite materials containing quantities of active materials, carbon conductors, and binders.\nL599@P24: The active materials are signified as the large spheres in Figure 2.3.\nL600@P24: The schematic in Figure 2.2a is not to scale since the thickness of the electrodes (µm length-scale)\nL601@P24: is much smaller than the length of the cell (cm length-scale) in a real cell. The schematic\nL602@P24: demonstrates how the separator overlaps the electrodes to prevent shorting. The exact dimensions\nL603@P24: of this overlap (and dimensions for the electrodes) are discussed in more detail in Section 5.2.5.\nL604@P24: The schematic also accentuates how the positive and negative foils extend past the coated electrode\nL605@P24: regions to provide current collectors.\nL606@P24: In the cell, tens to hundreds of bi-cell layers are stacked on top of each other and welded to positive\nL607@P24: and negative tabs through the current collectors (see Figure 2.2b). The entire, welded stack is then\nL608@P24: inserted into a stiff pouch composed of polymer-coated aluminum, filled with electrolyte, and\nL609@P24: sealed. The positive and negative tabs extend out of the sealed pouch for electrical connection (see\nL610@P24-25: Figure 2.2c).7\nL611@P25: Figure 2.2. Schematic of the stiff pouch cell design showing a) a single bi-cell layer of electrodes\nL612@P25: and separators, b) a full electrode stack and tabs that will be welded to the current collectors, and\nL613@P25: c) the final stiff pouch cell containing the electrode stack and filled with electrolyte. Images are\nL614@P25: drawn to accentuate features. Thicknesses in a) and b) are not to scale.\nL615@P25: Figure 2.3. Detailed schematic of a bi-cell layer within the pouch cell.\nL616@P25: separator\nL617@P25: negative\nL618@P25: electrode\nL619@P25: + – +\nL620@P25: Li+\nL621@P25: positive\nL729@P31: with information on how to alter the inputs and read the results.\nL730@P31: 3.2.1 Dashboard Ribbon\nL731@P31: The top ribbon of ‘Dashboard’ is frozen in place (Figure 3.3). One of the most important features\nL732@P31: of the ribbon is the ability to reset the calculation, which is located at the bottom. A reset of the\nL733@P31: calculation is recommended after changing design inputs. It is also required to re-converge the\nL734@P31-32: model if the output cells show a #N/A error. To reset the calculator:14\nL735@P32: If Macros are Enabled: CTRL + SHIFT + R\nL767@P33: the table of contents. Only the ‘Dashboard’ ribbon contains the calculation restart cell; however,\nL768@P33: the calculation can be restarted on any tab using the macro-enabled method (Crtl+Shift+R).\nL769@P33: Figure 3.4. Table of contents at the top of the 'Dashboard' (and other) worksheets for quick\nL770@P33: navigation.\nL771@P33: 3.2.2 Dashboard Inputs\nL772@P33: 3.2.2.1 Chemistry Inputs\nL773@P33: The inputs in ‘Dashboard’ are separated into three main categories: chemistry, battery design, and\nL1259@P51: It is possible, although highly unlikely, that the user may select a set of input variables that does\nL1260@P51: not immediately lead to a solution. In that case, the user should consider adjusting the convergence\nL1261@P51: constants and initial estimates for that study. Recall that most issues with BatPaC can be solved\nL1262@P51: by resetting the calculation (refer to Section 3.2.1). When the calculation is reset, BatPaC sets the\nL1263@P51: three parameters to their initial estimates. Then, the iterative solver in excel (see Section 3.1)\nL1301@P53: 4.1.2.1 Step 1: Choose couple and formation information\nL1302@P53: First, the active materials in the new couple must be chosen from an existing database in BatPaC.\nL1303@P53: This can be accomplished either by taking the values from the currently selected chemistry couple,\nL1304@P53: which was selected on ‘Dashboard’ (Figure 4.2a) or by creating a new couple from the database\nL1305@P53: of active materials provided by BatPaC (Figure 4.2b). Use the drop-down menu next to the “Select\nL1457@P60: charging requirements can be included in the design process. It is only TRUE for EVs.\nL1458@P60: Ramifications of charging requirements are discussed in Section 6. Item 10, “Ideal chemistry\nL1459@P60: couple,” provides guidance for the type of electrode couple best suited for the vehicle (see Section\nL1460@P60: 3.2.2.1). A warning is triggered if a different couple type is selected (see Section 3.2.3.1). The final\nL1461@P60: consideration in this set determines the added cost for multiple packs in the battery system.\nL1488@P61: representing a single set of configuration data for all seven batteries (Figure 4.9). The first, left\u0002most table in the worksheet contains the current selection by the default configuration macro. This\nL1489@P61: table should not be modified. As a reminder, most of the cells are purple indicating they are\nL1490@P61: automatic inputs that should not be adjusted directly (see Section 3.2.1). All other tables to the\nL1491@P61: right contain configurations for each vehicle type. Each vehicle type has its own set (i.e., columns)\nL1568@P66: connections, and packaging of the battery pack. The structural inputs reflect the battery design\nL1569@P66: presented in Section 2.1. They refer to physical constraints at the cell-, module-, and pack-level.\nL1570@P66: Cell constraints set the design of the non-electrochemically active portion of the cell – e.g.,\nL1571@P66: thicknesses of the container, length-to-width ratio of the electrodes, and size of the cell terminals.\nL1572@P66: The module constraints determine how the cells are packaged together – e.g., number of cells per\nL1585@P66: performance inputs. The main design outputs are the thickness of the electrodes, the electrode area,\nL1586@P66: and the number of bi-cell layers per cell. These values set the total dimensions of the cell (Section\nL1587@P66: 5.2.5), which, in turn, set the dimensions of the module (Section 5.3) and pack (Section 5.4).\nL1588@P66: 5.2.1 Governing Equations\nL1589@P66: The cell design model consists of five coupled equations that translate the cell geometry (i.e.,\nL1658@P68: the ASI in calculations associated with the rated power and energy. This section introduces the\nL1659@P68: ASI model employed in BatPaC and explains the physical meanings of the model components and\nL1660@P68: approximations. Detailed derivation of the model, as well as its validation against experiments,\nL1661@P68: can be found in [5].\nL1662@P68: 5.2.2.1 Experimental Definition of ASI\nL1663@P68: ASI is the impedance per unit area of electrode coating, generalized as a function of time, current\nL2476@P95: maximum allowable temperatures listed above. This dataset was diverse and included power and\nL2477@P95: energy couples; lithium titanate and graphite negative electrodes; and layered oxide and spin